<DocsLayout
  title="Mobile App"
  subtitle="A complete guide to running and managing nikcli from the Expo app in packages/mobile against a host server."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="overview" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      The nikcli mobile app is an Expo / React Native client that drives a running
      <strong>nikcli mobile host</strong> over its <code>/mobile/*</code> HTTP + SSE API. You start a
      host on your machine (or a cloud platform), pair the app with a token, and then work from your
      phone: run agent sessions, approve tool actions, review diffs, import GitHub repos, manage
      routines, and open interactive terminals.
    </p>
    <p>The app is organized into five tabs plus Settings and Profile:</p>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-8">
      <DocCard title="Sessions" eyebrow="Core"><p>Create and resume agent sessions with live SSE streaming, plan/code modes, approvals, and diffs.</p></DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Repos" eyebrow="Workspaces"><p>Server worktrees and GitHub repos — import, sandbox, and launch GitHub-backed sessions.</p></DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Routines" eyebrow="Automation"><p>Create, run, pause, and resume scheduled or API-triggered routines.</p></DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Terminal" eyebrow="Shell"><p>Fully interactive PTYs running on the host, with multiple tabs and copy/paste.</p></DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Settings & Profile" eyebrow="Management"><p>Providers, connectors, MCP, skills, agents, commands, memories, tokens, plugins, and account.</p></DocCard>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="host-setup" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Host setup</h2>
    <p>
      The app connects to a host process exposing the mobile API. Two commands cover the common
      cases. The server binds to <code>0.0.0.0</code> by default.
    </p>
    <h3>Start and pair in one step</h3>
    <p>Use this to start the host and immediately print a pairing token + QR code.</p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
# Local network / Tailscale — no --public-url needed
nikcli mobile serve --pair --pair-name iphone

# Public endpoint (Railway, Fly, etc.)
nikcli mobile serve \
  --hostname 0.0.0.0 \
  --port $PORT \
  --public-url https://your-host.example.com \
  --pair --pair-name iphone --pair-expiry-days 90
    </DocCode>
    <h3>Pair an already-running server</h3>
    <p>
      Use this to mint a new token for a server that is already up — e.g. to add a second device.
      <code>--public-url</code> is required so the QR encodes the right address.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
nikcli mobile pair \
  --public-url https://your-host.example.com \
  --name iphone --expiry-days 90 \
  --directory /home/user/projects/myrepo
    </DocCode>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Applies to</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>--hostname</code> / <code>--port</code></td><td>serve</td><td>Bind address and port. Defaults to <code>0.0.0.0</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--public-url</code></td><td>serve, pair</td><td>HTTPS URL the app uses from outside your LAN/tailnet. Required for <code>pair</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--pair</code></td><td>serve</td><td>Create a pairing token at startup and print token + QR.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--pair-name</code> / <code>--name</code></td><td>serve / pair</td><td>Label for the generated token.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--pair-expiry-days</code> / <code>--expiry-days</code></td><td>serve / pair</td><td>Optional token lifetime in days. Omit for a non-expiring token.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--directory</code></td><td>pair</td><td>Default working directory encoded into the QR; pre-fills the app's directory field.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <h3>Combined terminal + mobile host</h3>
    <p>
      <code>nikcli remote start --mobile</code> starts the mobile host alongside a terminal remote
      session (or <code>--mobile-only</code> for just the mobile host) and prints a pairing QR. It
      shares the same <code>--public-url</code>, <code>--pair</code>, and pairing flags.
    </p>
    <h3>Token management</h3>
    <DocCode language="bash">
nikcli mobile token list           # list issued pairing tokens
nikcli mobile token revoke &lt;id&gt;     # revoke a token by ID
    </DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Public hosts must be secured">
      <p>
        Public or LAN-facing hosting requires <code>NIKCLI_SERVER_PASSWORD</code> to be set. The
        server refuses to start with a non-loopback address or <code>--public-url</code> unless this
        variable is present (or Tailscale auth is active).
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="connect" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Connect the app</h2>
    <p>
      With no saved server, the app opens the <strong>Connect</strong> screen (root route
      <code>/</code>) — a "Secure pairing" card with three fields and a
      <strong>Validate and continue</strong> button.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Required</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Server URL</strong></td><td>Yes</td><td>Base address of the running host, e.g. <code>https://your-host.example.com</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Bearer token</strong></td><td>No</td><td>Pairing token; sent as <code>Authorization: Bearer …</code> on every request.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Default server directory</strong></td><td>No</td><td>Server path that scopes file/git/session operations; sent as the <code>x-nikcli-directory</code> header.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Tapping <strong>Validate and continue</strong> calls <code>GET /mobile/bootstrap</code> with
      the entered credentials. On success the config is saved to the device secure store and the app
      routes to <code>/sessions</code> if a user token already exists, otherwise to <code>/login</code>.
      On failure an inline error banner shows the server's message. On later launches the app
      re-pings the saved host and skips Connect automatically when it responds.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="deep-link" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Deep link & QR</h2>
    <p>
      Scanning the pairing QR opens a <code>nikcli://</code> URL that pre-fills the Connect fields.
      The parser accepts two equivalent forms:
    </p>
    <DocCode language="text">
nikcli://connect?server=&lt;url&gt;&amp;token=&lt;token&gt;&amp;directory=&lt;dir&gt;
nikcli://?server=&lt;url&gt;&amp;token=&lt;token&gt;&amp;directory=&lt;dir&gt;
    </DocCode>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Required</th><th>Maps to</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>server</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Server URL</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>token</code></td><td>No</td><td>Bearer token</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>directory</code></td><td>No</td><td>Default server directory</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Deep links are handled both at cold start and while the app is open, so scanning with the app
      already running also works. URLs with a non-<code>nikcli:</code> scheme are ignored.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="login" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Login & accounts</h2>
    <p>
      After connecting, the app shows the <strong>Login</strong> screen. This is a second auth layer:
      the pairing token authenticates the <em>device</em> to the host, while the user account token
      authenticates the <em>person</em> and scopes their sessions and data.
    </p>
    <p>
      The screen calls <code>GET /user/status</code> first. If no accounts exist yet it shows only a
      <strong>Create account</strong> form; otherwise it offers <strong>Sign in</strong> and
      <strong>Create account</strong>. Sign-in needs email + password; registration needs username,
      email, and a password of at least 8 characters (display name optional). A
      <strong>Remember me</strong> toggle (on by default) stores only your email — never the password
      or token — and surfaces it as a tappable chip next time.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Created by</th><th>Sent as</th><th>Controls</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Pairing token</td><td><code>nikcli mobile pair</code> / <code>serve --pair</code></td><td><code>Authorization: Bearer</code> + <code>x-nikcli-directory</code></td><td>Device access to the host API</td></tr>
        <tr><td>User session token</td><td><code>/user/login</code> or <code>/user/register</code></td><td>Managed by the app for user-scoped endpoints</td><td>Identity, roles, per-user data</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Stored securely">
      <p>Both tokens are kept in <code>expo-secure-store</code> (iOS Keychain / Android Keystore), never in plain preferences.</p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="sessions" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Sessions list</h2>
    <p>
      The Sessions tab is the landing screen. A hero "Operations board" card doubles as the create
      entry point and a live status summary, with chips for total sessions, busy, retry,
      GitHub-linked, container-backed, the active provider/model, and the current project.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Search</strong> — a debounced field filters sessions, repos, or branches via the host.</li>
      <li><strong>New session</strong> — calls the host to create a session (using your configured execution target: local worktree or container sandbox) and opens it. Title, agent, and model are adjusted from inside the session.</li>
      <li><strong>List items</strong> — show title, location (GitHub repo→branch or local path), an IDLE/BUSY/RETRY badge, file and additions/deletions chips, and an Open button.</li>
      <li><strong>Actions</strong> — long-press or the overflow button opens a sheet: Open, Abort (when busy), or Delete (optimistic, re-fetches on error).</li>
      <li><strong>Pull-to-refresh</strong> reloads the list.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="chat" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Chat & composer</h2>
    <p>
      Inside a session the screen is a scrollable transcript above a fixed composer. The transcript
      auto-follows new output while you are near the bottom; scrolling up pauses auto-follow.
    </p>
    <h3>Composer</h3>
    <p>The composer capsule grows from 2 to 6 lines and exposes a toolbar:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Paperclip</strong> — attach files (staged and sent as data-URL file parts).</li>
      <li><strong>Terminal icon</strong> — open the command palette (grouped: View, Session, Compose, GitHub, Memories, Presets, Skills, MCP, Subtasks, Commands).</li>
      <li><strong>Git branch icon</strong> — open the Git review panel.</li>
      <li><strong>Plus</strong> — open the tool drawer (models, skills, tools, MCP servers).</li>
      <li><strong>Plan / Code toggle</strong> — a sliding segmented control.</li>
      <li>Model label pill, optional <strong>MCP N</strong> badge, character count, and Send / Stop button.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Plan vs code mode</h3>
    <p>
      In <strong>Plan</strong> mode the host is asked to analyze and propose an approach without
      making changes until you confirm; in <strong>Code</strong> mode the message is sent as-is. The
      mode can be switched mid-session and its default is set in Settings.
    </p>
    <h3>Slash commands & sending</h3>
    <p>
      Typing <code>/</code> shows up to five matching commands with SKILL / MCP / TASK / arg badges;
      tapping inserts the command. Plain messages stream the assistant reply over SSE
      (<code>/mobile/session/:id/stream</code>) with reasoning, text, tool cards, and — when present —
      a per-message cost and token count. The chosen model is only sent on the first message so the
      host keeps its running model afterward. If a send fails offline, the input is restored and the
      operation is queued for the next foreground.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="tool-calls" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Tool calls</h2>
    <p>
      Each tool call renders as a collapsible card whose status dot cycles pending → running →
      completed/error as stream events arrive. The collapsed header shows a status dot, a
      tool-specific icon (terminal, file, folder, search, globe, or wrench), the tool name and
      title, a status pill, and an elapsed-time badge.
    </p>
    <p>
      Expanding the card reveals the <strong>Input</strong> key/value pairs and the
      <strong>Output</strong> (or Error) — truncated to 400 characters with a "Show all" toggle and a
      Copy button for the full text.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="approvals" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Approvals & permissions</h2>
    <p>
      The app surfaces two approval flows that map to the host permission system: tool-execution
      <strong>permission requests</strong> and structured <strong>question requests</strong>.
    </p>
    <h3>Permission cards</h3>
    <p>
      Pending permissions render above the transcript with the requested permission, an optional
      description, command/path scope chips, the matching glob patterns, and three actions —
      <strong>Reject</strong>, <strong>Allow once</strong>, <strong>Always allow</strong>. A compact
      approval bar above the composer mirrors these actions and lets you page through queued requests.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Always allow is temporary">
      <p>"Always allow" is scoped on the host and persists only until nikcli restarts — it is not a permanent on-disk policy.</p>
    </DocCallout>
    <h3>Questions</h3>
    <p>
      Question requests show the prompt and option pills. Single-select submits on tap; multi-select
      uses checkboxes and a submit arrow. A reject button dismisses the question. New permission and
      question events also fire a local notification (deduplicated) and a haptic.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="files-diffs" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Files & diffs</h2>
    <h3>Patch diffs</h3>
    <p>
      When an assistant message includes a patch, a "Load diff" button fetches the file diffs and
      renders one collapsible card per file with an added/deleted/modified badge and
      <code>+N / -N</code> counts. Expanding shows a syntax-tinted line diff (additions green,
      removals red, context muted).
    </p>
    <h3>File explorer</h3>
    <p>
      The folder icon in the session header opens the <strong>Repository tree</strong> for the
      session's workspace: a filterable file tree with git status markers (A / M / D), workspace text
      search (ripgrep-backed), fuzzy filename filtering, expand/collapse, and stat chips for files,
      dirs, open, and changed.
    </p>
    <h3>Editor</h3>
    <p>
      Tapping a file opens an in-app editor with syntax highlighting, find-in-file (with case
      toggle and match navigation), word-wrap, copy, and a View/Edit toggle (large files over ~5,000
      lines open read-only). Edits are tracked locally; <strong>Save</strong> writes the file back to
      the host, and leaving with unsaved changes prompts to Save, Discard, or Cancel.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="repos" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Repos & GitHub</h2>
    <p>
      The Repos tab is a unified workspace portfolio: the <em>server worktrees</em> the host knows
      about, and the <em>GitHub repos</em> from your connected account. A summary card shows live
      counts, the active worktree, the execution target, and your GitHub login.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Connect GitHub first">
      <p>
        GitHub browsing, import, and launch require GitHub to be connected (OAuth device flow or a
        personal access token in Settings → GitHub). Until then the GitHub section shows a "Connect
        GitHub first" empty state.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Server worktrees</strong> — tap a worktree to make it the active directory (forwarded as the <code>x-nikcli-directory</code> header). The active one is highlighted.</li>
      <li><strong>New sandbox</strong> — provision a fresh isolated server worktree before starting a task.</li>
      <li><strong>Import a repo</strong> — "Import only" clones the repo to the host and sets it active without creating a session; "Use repo" switches to an already-imported clone.</li>
      <li><strong>Guided launch</strong> — "Configure session" loads branches and opens a 3-step wizard (repo → branch → launch) to create a worktree and session in one go via the host GitHub API.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Git review</h3>
    <p>
      Git operations are exposed through the Git review panel launched from a session: view
      <strong>status</strong> and <strong>diff</strong>, browse <strong>commits</strong> and
      <strong>branches</strong>, <strong>stage</strong>/<strong>unstage</strong>/<strong>discard</strong>
      files, <strong>commit</strong> and <strong>push</strong>, and — for GitHub-backed sessions —
      <strong>publish a pull request</strong> or <strong>clean up</strong> the worktree. A status bar
      widget shows branch, staged/unstaged/untracked counts, and ahead/behind. Discard affects only
      tracked files.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="routines" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Routines</h2>
    <p>
      The Routines tab is a full create/read/update/delete interface for saved prompts that run as
      sessions — on a cron schedule, via an HTTP webhook, or on demand. The list shows active/paused
      counts; each row shows the name, a prompt preview, a status chip, a trigger summary (cron and/or
      <code>API</code>, or "manual only"), the last-run time, and <strong>Run now</strong> /
      <strong>Edit</strong> buttons.
    </p>
    <p>The detail screen edits everything:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Name</strong> and <strong>Prompt</strong> (both required).</li>
      <li><strong>Schedule</strong> — preset chips (@hourly, Every 6h, @daily, @weekly) or a custom cron expression, with an enable/disable toggle.</li>
      <li><strong>API trigger</strong> — shows the endpoint <code>POST /mobile/routines/trigger/&lt;token&gt;</code> (no bearer token needed) with an enable/disable toggle.</li>
      <li><strong>Run now / Pause / Resume / Delete</strong>, plus a link to the last run's session.</li>
    </ul>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Also on the CLI">
      <p>
        The API trigger token is assigned by the host when the routine is created with
        <code>--api</code> / <code>--api-token</code>. Everything here is also available via
        <code>nikcli routine</code> — see <a href="/docs/routines">Routines</a>.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="terminal" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Terminal</h2>
    <p>
      The Terminal tab opens <strong>fully interactive PTYs</strong> running on the host — you can
      type commands, use arrow keys, run editors, and see color output. Each tab is a terminal
      emulator in a WebView connected to the host over a WebSocket; the auth token and active
      directory are passed as query parameters (WebSockets can't send custom headers), and resizes are
      synced back to the server PTY.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Multiple tabs</strong> stay connected in the background; only the active one is visible.</li>
      <li><strong>Toolbar</strong> shows the working directory and offers Copy (selection or visible text), Paste (sends clipboard into the PTY), New tab, and Close all.</li>
      <li><strong>Reconnect</strong> — up to five automatic retries before a "Connection Failed" overlay with a Retry button.</li>
    </ul>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Server-local & non-persistent">
      <p>
        The PTY runs on the host (no local shell on the device), and tab state is not persisted across
        app restarts — though host PTYs may keep running until they time out or are cleaned up.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="settings" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Settings</h2>
    <p>
      Settings is a single scrollable control plane; each section can be shown or hidden per device
      from Appearance. Some areas open dedicated sub-screens. This is where you manage everything the
      host exposes.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>What you manage</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Models / Providers</strong></td><td>Browse the host provider catalog, pick a default model for new mobile sessions, and save or remove provider API keys on the host.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>GitHub</strong></td><td>Connect via OAuth device flow (or save the OAuth client ID), view the connected login, disconnect, or save a manual <code>ghp_…</code> token.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>MCP</strong></td><td>Add remote (URL) or local (command) MCP servers; enable/disable, connect, authenticate (browser), or clear auth, with live status chips.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Connectors</strong></td><td>Save/remove credentials for Figma, Slack, Discord, Microsoft Teams, Linear, Lovable, and Google Chat. Stored on the host.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Skills</strong></td><td>Browse the host skill registry (sources: <code>.nikcli</code>, <code>.claude</code>, <code>.agents</code>) with name, category, version, and tags.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Agents</strong></td><td>Browse built-in and custom agents with their tools and IDs; the default agent is marked.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Commands</strong></td><td>Add/edit/remove host slash commands (template, optional agent/model override, subtask flag); manage local prompt presets and composer defaults.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Memories</strong></td><td>Add/remove reusable host-backed snippets and browse recent prompt history.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Tokens</strong></td><td>Create long-lived mobile bearer tokens (name + expiry), copy them once, view last-used/expiry, and revoke instantly.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Plugins</strong></td><td>Save API keys for Codex (openai), Cursor, and GitHub Copilot on the host.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Connection</strong></td><td>Server URL, bearer token, default directory; Save connection, Forget server, Sign out.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Execution</strong></td><td>Execution target: local worktree (default) or container sandbox (when Docker/Podman is available).</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="profile" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Profile & account</h2>
    <p>
      The Profile screen shows your identity (avatar, display name, <code>@username</code>, email,
      role, member-since), account stats, and an edit form for display name and password. Admins
      additionally get a <strong>User management</strong> list to promote/revoke admins and delete
      accounts. A <strong>Sign out</strong> action (with confirmation) ends the session.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Deletions are permanent">
      <p>Deleting a user from the admin panel removes the account permanently on the host and cannot be undone.</p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="notifications" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Notifications & Live Activities</h2>
    <p>
      Notifications are <strong>off by default</strong>. Enable them in Settings → Interaction →
      Notifications; the app requests OS permission on enable. Three kinds gate on individual toggles:
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Toggle</th><th>Fires when</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>sessionReady</code></td><td>Session ready</td><td>A session goes idle / ready for the next command.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>permissions</code></td><td>Permission requests</td><td>A session is waiting for an approval.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>failures</code></td><td>Failures</td><td>A session enters an error state.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Notifications are suppressed while the app is foregrounded and deduplicated within an 8-second
      window. Android uses a dedicated <code>nikcli-mobile</code> channel. On iOS 16.2+ the app drives
      <strong>Live Activities</strong> that show session state on the Lock Screen / Dynamic Island,
      with a color tone derived from state (attention for approvals, countdown for retries, editorial
      for GitHub publish/cleanup, work for running). Activities deep-link back into the session and are
      reconciled on app foreground. Live Activities are iOS-only.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="offline" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Offline behavior</h2>
    <p>
      When a send can't reach the host, the message is queued locally (in secure storage) and replayed
      automatically the next time the app returns to the foreground. The queue is capped at 50 entries
      (oldest evicted) and drained with per-operation retry — successful sends are removed, failures
      stay for the next attempt.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="themes" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Themes & preferences</h2>
    <p>
      Settings → Appearance offers a color mode (System, Light, Dark) and a color theme picker with
      100+ named themes (Nikcli default, plus Nord, Dracula, Tokyo Night, Catppuccin, Gruvbox,
      Solarized, GitHub, One Dark, Kanagawa, Rose Pine, and many more). Mode and theme apply instantly
      and persist on the device.
    </p>
    <p>Other persisted preferences you can manage in Settings include:</p>
